WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.440
How many wings do they have? Are they solid? Do&nbsp;
they eat? Do they have free will? For centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.200
philosophers and theologians have debated&nbsp;
these and other burning questions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.960
the mysterious celestial beings known as angels.
As beings that mediate between heaven and earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.880
angels have been the focus of both&nbsp;
fascination and fear for centuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.920
They’ve been described as&nbsp;
fireballs, forms without substance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.400
a set of interlocking golden wheels, and beings&nbsp;
that take up so much space that it would take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.120
bird seven hundred years to fly across their&nbsp;
bodies. They’ve been shown collecting blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.480
sprouting out of the tails of heavenly&nbsp;
steeds, and trumpeting the end of time.&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.360
But for many of us, the enduring image of&nbsp;
an angel is a winged, benevolent baby or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.840
adult - possibly bathed in golden light, sporting&nbsp;
a halo, and wielding a musical instrument.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.600
So where did this archetype come from, and why did&nbsp;
we get rid of all the scary (aka awesome) stuff?

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.080
In Abrahamic religions and/or cosmology,&nbsp;
angels are heavenly creatures who act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.600
as messengers between heaven and&nbsp;
earth. As the mouthpieces of God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.480
they act in accordance with His will – most of the&nbsp;
time, but we’ll get to that later. They also– tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.040
to show up at spiritually significant events.
There’s little consensus on the appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.440
substance, or character of angels across&nbsp;
Abrahamic scripture. In Jewish, Christian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.760
and Islamic writings, angels take numerous&nbsp;
forms and play many different roles:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.560
from acting as envoys and soldiers to&nbsp;
providing transport and making the clouds move.&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.038
But one story that’s common to all three religions&nbsp;
is the appearance of angels to the prophet&nbsp;Abraham. 

00:01:30.038 --> 00:01:34.080
First surfacing in ancient Hebrew texts,&nbsp;
the story tells of three angels who visited the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.200
old man. They told him to leave his home and&nbsp;
found a new nation and spiritual community, thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.080
laying the foundation for Abrahamic religions.
From this common story, angels appear throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.560
Abrahamic writings in a variety of roles.&nbsp;
Metatron, who appears in the Talmud and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.760
throughout Judaic traditions, is seen as the&nbsp;
angelic scribe who keeps track of human deeds.&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.640
Other named angels like Michael and Gabriel&nbsp;
appear throughout the Old Testament as messengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:04.000
while smaller angels called cherubim are said to&nbsp;
guard the Garden of Eden. In Islamic tradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.200
the angel Jibril reveals the word of Allah and&nbsp;
inspiration for the Quran to the Prophet Muhammad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.400
a descendent of Abraham. The Quran names&nbsp;
several more angels like Munkar and Nakir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.960
who question the dead about their deeds.
These are only a few of many angelic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.520
characters who rear their brilliant heads&nbsp;
throughout Abrahamic cosmology. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.480
there’s little agreement on their appearance.&nbsp;
Some sources focus on their great size, describing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.040
their bodies stretching across the horizon,&nbsp;
reaching from heaven to earth, or proportionate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.920
to one third of the globe. Others describe them&nbsp;
as having human aspects, being constructed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.280
light – or having no physical body at all.
As awesome, possibly intangible beings who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.880
may or may not be seen with a naked&nbsp;
human eye, angels posed quite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.440
dilemma for artists. Rather than attempting&nbsp;
to represent abstract spiritual entities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.240
artists came to use different symbols to denote&nbsp;
an angel – some of which remain familiar today.&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.240
Pop culture might have you thinking that&nbsp;
angels earn their wings through good deeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.400
and other escapades– or in the case of the&nbsp;
dogs in All Dogs Go To Heaven, because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.001
are inherently all very good boys and girls.
But the image of a winged guardian predates&nbsp;&nbsp;Hollywood. 

00:03:10.001 --> 00:03:14.480
While Abraham’s visitors aren’t winged,&nbsp;
there are numerous references to angels’ swift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:19.440
flight and vast wings throughout scripture – in&nbsp;
the Quran, they appear with up to four pairs.&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.160
Scholars have linked the trope of the divine&nbsp;
flying guardian to giant winged protector deities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.360
found in Mesopotamian art and architecture, or&nbsp;
ancient defender goddesses like Isis of Egypt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.880
Nike of Greece. In art and architecture, wings&nbsp;
were a useful way to distinguish divine beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.240
from mortals. They acted as a visual shorthand for&nbsp;
swiftness, ethereality, and supernatural power.&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.360
In Islamic illustrations, angels often&nbsp;
appear with multicolored wings and robes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.040
and are thought to have been influenced&nbsp;
by ancient East Asian symbolism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.120
From the first to the third centuries, angels&nbsp;
in Christian and Jewish art were as likely to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.720
bearded or balding men rather than flying beings.
But the popularity of winged angels in art truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.560
began to take off with the legalization&nbsp;
of Christianity in the fourth century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.280
Angels were typically coded male to distinguish&nbsp;
them from ancient, winged goddesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.720
and can be seen across sarcophagi,&nbsp;
mosaics, paintings, and sculptures.&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.840
Even more so than wings, a golden or burning&nbsp;
discus was used to denote divinity since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.200
ancient times. Images of sacred and royal figures&nbsp;
crowned with a band of light are common across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.520
Persian, Buddhist and Hindu art, and sun&nbsp;
deities like the Zoroastrian Mithra and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:30.720
Egyptian Ra were crowned with golden discs.
Centuries later, the Roman sun God Helios was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.280
often depicted with a band of gold around his&nbsp;
head – along with the Roman Emperor, who held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.080
divine status. This tradition continued with&nbsp;
the conversion of the emperors to Christianity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.280
Over time, what became known as the halo – from&nbsp;
the Greek halos, “disk of the sun or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.640
moon” – was used to represent the superhuman&nbsp;
holiness of God, saints, and angels alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.280
While Medieval angels sported fat, opulent&nbsp;
halos, as realist art became more popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.240
from the Renaissance on, halos began to shrink .
In addition to their feathery wings and shining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.960
heads, angels are often depicted with some&nbsp;
sort of musical proclivity. Early scripture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.600
doesn’t note their musical talent – but in later&nbsp;
texts including the New Testament and the Quran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.520
angels ring in Judgment Day with their&nbsp;
trumpets. The angel Gabriel might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.000
the most famous trumpeter, often depicted&nbsp;
alerting mortals of God’s return to earth.&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.800
Singing and making music was&nbsp;
associated with praising God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.440
leading to countless depictions of angels with&nbsp;
harps, lyres, horns, and trumpets. Some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.440
most famous examples include medieval&nbsp;
musicians praising the mother and child.&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.400
Alongside these visual attempts, writers and&nbsp;
philosophers were also clamoring to classify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.120
the nature of angels. In his treatise on&nbsp;
angels, the Egyptian polymath al-Suyūṭī&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.640
argued that the angels were made of fire or light.&nbsp;
In contrast, the Persian physician Ibn Sīna viewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:50.080
angels as intellectual forces without bodies.
Other thinkers created hierarchies of angels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.600
separating flurries of celestial&nbsp;
forms into specific groups and roles.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.800
Angelic hierarchies and taxonomies were intended&nbsp;
to nail down the purpose of these mind-bending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.160
beings – but also to discern the mysterious&nbsp;
ways in which God works. By assigning specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.480
roles to heavenly beings, theologians were&nbsp;
attempting to lend order to the cosmic universe.&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.160
A famous example is a fifth century text by the&nbsp;
Christian monk Dionysus, who divided “Heavenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.440
Beings” into nine ranks. Subdivided into three&nbsp;
groups of three, the angels were classified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.720
in terms of physical proximity to the Lord.
In the first hierarchy, we have the Cherubim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.760
Seraphim, and the thrones orbiting around the&nbsp;
lord. Described as “many eyed and many winged”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:35.120
these angels are in perpetual motion. The Thrones&nbsp;
are the most ambiguous. They’ve been understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.240
as guardians of the Lord’s throne, but also as&nbsp;
burning rings that form the wheels of His chariot.&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.360
In the middle group of angels, we have&nbsp;
Dominations, Powers, and Authorities. They rarely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.360
appeared to humans, but were thought to take&nbsp;
care of matters like miracles and fighting evil.&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.800
Finally, we have Principalities, Archangels,&nbsp;
and – simply – angels. They often have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.280
specific names and roles, like Michael as&nbsp;
guardian of the Jewish people and Gabriel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.440
as an angel that enlightens chosen humans.
In the twelfth century, Medieval Jewish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.800
philosopher Moses Maimonides’ classified angels&nbsp;
into ten groups, ranked in accordance with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.440
understanding of God including man-like&nbsp;
messengers, scribes, and angels of motion.&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:19.280
The appeal of a cosmic – if rather bureaucratic -&nbsp;
order also infused culture throughout the Middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.480
Ages, with many writers and artists attempting to&nbsp;
map the spiritual world. Written in the fourteenth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.760
century, Dante’s Divine Comedy is a trio of epic&nbsp;
poems that lead the reader through the realms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:35.360
Hell, Purgatory, and Paradise. His vision of&nbsp;
angels reinforced Dionysus’ hierarchy for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.000
popular audience and was another important&nbsp;
cultural attempt to decode heavenly realms.&nbsp;

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.600
But there’s one class of angel&nbsp;
that’s decidedly not celestial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:49.200
The time has come to talk about fallen angels.
Fallen angels are those who have been expelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.120
from heaven due to sin or plotting against&nbsp;
God. Going all the way back to 200-100 BCE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:59.920
the Jewish book of Enoch described a group of&nbsp;
angels who descended to earth to mate with women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.720
and teach them divination. Their offspring&nbsp;
were giant cannibals who unleashed war and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.720
want among humankind. Thus angels who stray from&nbsp;
the status quo often breed further chaos on earth.&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:15.360
The Enoch story has been compared to&nbsp;
Islamic accounts of Harut and Marut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.480
two angels who taught humanity about witchcraft.&nbsp;
The Quran also tells the story of a high-ranking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:25.760
angel who was ejected from heaven when he refused&nbsp;
to bow to Adam. Banished to the underworld, Iblis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:31.200
retains an ability to influence humankind for the&nbsp;
worse, whispering evil deeds to tempt us into sin.&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.200
The idea of a spiritual war between the forces&nbsp;
of good and evil comes to one spectacular head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.160
in the Christian Book of Revelation, where evil&nbsp;
angels were cast out of heaven alongside the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:47.680
evil angel of all – a great black dragon, known&nbsp;
as Satan. Medieval artists were inspired by this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.880
particular fallen angel, often depicting him as&nbsp;
a grotesquely inverted angel with scaley wings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.480
curled toenails and horns.
Through the Renaissance, fallen angels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.840
signaled the dangers of succumbing to temptation&nbsp;
and vice in Christian art. But, to some, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:08.000
were figures who questioned authority and embodied&nbsp;
free will. In his 1667 epic poem Paradise Lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.520
John Milton portrays Lucifer as a freethinking&nbsp;
angel who rages against an authoritarian God.&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:17.520
This idea of the fallen angel as a tragic&nbsp;
antihero resonated with many artists of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.960
the Romantic period, who portrayed him as a&nbsp;
(surprisingly hunky) victim of his own idealism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.560
In their many iterations, fallen angels&nbsp;
straddle the fragile line between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:30.720
profane and divine – and remind us that&nbsp;
even the “purest” beings can be corrupted.&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:36.640
As dignified messengers, cheeky babies, and&nbsp;
subterranean rebels, angels continue to blaze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.000
across our cultural imaginary. The fluffy-winged&nbsp;
guardian bathed in shining light is a hybrid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.760
Abrahamic tropes. But it’s also a vehicle for&nbsp;
exploring the joys and sorrows of life. In pop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.400
culture, angels often appear to humans at moments&nbsp;
when we’re questioning the world – acting as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:58.480
advisors, sidekicks, or guardians in trying times.
As the mediators between known and unknown worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.520
angels are powerful symbols&nbsp;
of our search for meaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:06.080
Since ancient times, they have appeared to clarify&nbsp;
people’s purpose in the world. But they’ve also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:17.840
been seen to question authority – reminding&nbsp;
us that the search for meaning is never over.

